ions  you know patterns of polarization and  preference formation okay and let's  define the term polarization just so  we're all on the same page right so the  way that I use that in my book it has  these two aspects so effective  polarization and then preference  polarization and I think of these things  as the extent to which groups like each  other how they get along and then the  extent to which groups disagree on  matters of substance so this comes you  know more out of the literature in  American politics where there is this  differentiation between you know  effective polarization where there's  something about feelings towards an out  group and then preferences and what I  find is both are really important even  if you have agreement and hate each  other it's sometimes hard to compromise  if you really like each other but  disagree quite a bit it's also hard to  compromise so these two different  aspects end up being really important in  tandem and what led you to write the  book yeah so I started graduate school  in 2011 I'd been traveling to the Middle  East and working on the Middle East  since 2005 as an undergraduate I went to  Cairo for a summer and it kind of  changed the course of my life at the  time what was happening was called the  kite in hindsight it's been called the  Cairo spring so there was this uptick in  mobilization people demanding certain  democratic rights and the state the  Egyptian state responded quite heavy  handily to this and since then you know  the way in which authoritarian regimes  and the repressive environment affect  political actors has been an interest of  mine when I started my dissertation in  2011-2012 I was interested in  polarization I hadn't quite connected it  with this interest in repression yet but  the more that I you know I went I went  back to Egypt I started working in  Tunisia the conversations I was having  with people really focused on this idea  of you know joint victimhood or you know  singular victimhood depending on the  country in Egypt you have a case where  the Brotherhood the Muslim  was targeted much worse than other  opposition groups in Tunisia you have  what I call a widespread repressive  environment everyone was in jail  together everyone was an exile together  and these stories just started emerging  where this was clearly foundational to  what came later and so why Tunisia and  why Egypt yeah so this is always a big  question of political science how you  choose your cases and my colleagues and  I sometimes joke sometimes you just  really like the country Egypt I you know  have a soft spot for it's been the place  I studied Arabic I spent quite a bit of  time there before I started my PhD but  Egypt in Tunisia were the first two Arab  Spring cases to go essentially so a long  term dictator and Tunisia resigned in  January 2011 in February 2011 the long  term dictator in Egypt resigned and you  know you know in many ways they had very  similar starts they were early cases  there was unprecedented mass  mobilization these to dictators resign  and then within three years they were in  very vastly different situations so  Tunisia was holding a next round of  elections it's passed the Constitution  you know it still has major security  issues and other you know  democratization is not an easy process  but it was continuing down that path and  in Egypt there was a coup and almost a  complete reversion back to  authoritarianism and so the combination  of the similarities at the beginning and  the divergent paths made it you know the  kind of puzzle you want to try to  explain so I am curious about your  methodology did you use the same  techniques in both countries or did you  mix it up tell us about that yeah so the  main methodology is based quite a bit on  you know qualitative evidence so this  includes over a hundred and twenty  interviews that I did with members of  the Opposition in both Egypt and Tunisia  in Egypt it was a little bit more  difficult in Tunisia I went to the  country I was there for about six months  doing these interviews it's not a  particularly large country so  you know between tunas and and some of  the major cities I was able to get a  good representative sample of the people  I wanted to talk to and I found people  quite open to talking about how their  experiences of prison and exile had  shaped them men and women or primarily  men mostly men just you know as a  function of who was in right politics  although there is quite a bit of  interesting work coming out about wives  of politicians who were also repressed  kind of as an extension of their husband  and then also female activists who had  been you know working under the previous  regime in Egypt because of the coup most  of the Muslim Brotherhood members have  either been arrested or you know have  fled to different parts of of the  country and so I didn't excuse me of the  globe and so I did interviews in  Istanbul in London to try and get you  know again a representative sample of  the kinds of people that I was  interested in how did you how did you  find them in Turkey and the other places  yeah so having worked on the Brotherhood  and Islamists more generally for a  couple of years now it was kind of  starting with one person and then what  might be called a snowball sample where  they introduce you to other people I  contacted a few lawyers I you know blind  tweeted at people today if they were  available but I think one thing that I I  felt as I was doing the the work was  that once I had a positive experience  with someone where you know they felt  like their story was being listened to  they were then more likely to recommend  you know someone else a friend or a  colleague do an interview with me as  well to get more of the story like I I  think people like to be listened to I  think they like to matter and these were  extremely traumatic formative  experiences and you know what I think is  great about you know the way that the  book has kind of played out is that it  highlights those experiences of  suffering and put some kind of meaning  onto it so I I think that was part of  why it ended up being maybe easier than  I thought although it was not easy  but once you know one person had a good  experience they were willing to  recommend me to someone else so I'm  curious based on your research did you  find similar things in both countries or  were they different in some way yeah so  they ended up being different  so in Tunisia as I mentioned the way  that the previous regime the Ben Ali  regime had repressed its opposition was  what I call in the book a widespread  repressive environments oh you know  communist Islamist leftists of different  sorts centrist anyone who was opposition  was working in opposition to the regime  would be arrested and targeted and so  you end up with very different kinds of  people very representing different kinds  of groups in jail together in exile  together quite a few of them ended up in  France and other European countries  there's also just a similarity of  experience so even if you know an  Islamist and a communist aren't in jail  together they were probably in very  similar circumstances their families  suffering in the same way and what I  find is that that creates some kind of  you know collective identity that  bridges across these different  opposition identities in Egypt there's a  it's a different story so the Mubarak  regime really just targets the Muslim  Brotherhood and it's not to say that  there isn't repression of other groups  but the Brotherhood certainly gets it  worse and more consistently worse and  why do you think that is yeah so that's  a big big question I guess of endogenous  we couldn't political science I'm  looking I'm now working on a project  with two other researchers at Yale  looking at basically this question why  do regimes repress in different ways and  we look at colonial investments and kind  of you know pre-independence investments  in things like the police in the  military which seem to have these  long-term downstream effects but the  idea is that you know depending on what  you inherit in terms of intelligence or  police capabilities determines how how  you can repress so you know the the  British system really leaves the  Egyptians with the ability to  distinguish between real and non real  threats  as a result they can kind of economize  and target the group that's actually a  threat the French system ends up leaving  in place a very diffuse and maybe not so  effective you know Intelligence Agency  and and institutions that are really  spying on each other rather than their  opposition and so you end up with an  intelligence agency that can't  distinguish between real and fake  threats so it's kind of everybody ends  up being repressed so that's a little  bit beyond what the book talks about but  it's it's always the logical question of  why is this different and it's I think  it's a really interesting question too  so I'm curious in in the when you're  doing the research and talking to people  what kind of questions are you asking  them yeah so just because it seems you  know very you know emotionally driven  kind of question right and I should say  these are long interviews so it's not  something where I show up at someone's  house and ask them so tell me about the  time you were tortured what I try to do  is start with basically the beginning of  their political lives so how did they  get interested in politics a lot of  times it has to do with a personal  experience or their parents and it's  probably you know 30 to 45 minutes into  a conversation about the narrative they  want to tell me of their life but then  we get into some of these you know more  difficult experiences so yes starting  with you know how did you get involved  often something will come up I was  arrested you know when I was politically  active in college or you know this  happened to me when I was young and that  kind of opens the door to starting to  ask about that and then you know what's  interesting too is that once that door  is open and once you've started talking  about kind of personally interesting  things they're also more willing to talk  about some of these other effects so the  effects on their wives the effects on  their children you know the way in which  their friendships were changed so most  these interviews were quite long three  four hours in people's homes in places  where they felt comfortable to talk  about this kind of you know deeply  personal experience  okay and based on your research what are  the implications for Tunisia and Egypt  yeah so the immediate implication was  you know the divergence we currently see  in Egypt in Tunisia so Tunisia as I  mentioned was capable of bringing  together it's old opposition they passed  a new constitution it did take three  years but they did it they held first  elections they've now held municipal  elections there's another set of  election scheduled for next year so you  know by political science standards  they're doing  all the things you need to do to get  into the next stage of democratic  consolidation in Egypt you know it's a  completely different story again there  was a coup there's been a reversion to  authoritarianism and so the book links  experiences a repression to these  developments through polarization so you  know a widespread repressive regime made  a case where there was less polarization  they were more willing and able to work  together on these important decisions  whereas in Egypt you have this targeted  repressive environment there was just  never that same level of cooperation or  agreement and it's really devolved since  2013 since the coup the Brotherhood at  this point is banned as a terrorist  organization as I mentioned you know  quite a few of their members are in jail  at this point I think up to 40,000  members so you know I what I find  interesting is how these things are  really linked to the historical legacies  of the authoritarian period right you  know I'm just thinking about Egypt in  particular and how the Brotherhood was  specifically targeted and I'm wondering  if that has a chilling effect on other  groups in Egypt saying hey you know if  we you know tried to uprise or form  together a group that they would you  know the regime would crack down on them  as they did on the Brotherhood yeah I  mean do you think there's any kind of  thing like that going on yeah at the  moment it there's definitely a chilling  effect the current regime I think has  broken out of even the mold  you know previous authoritarian regimes  in Egypt at last year during the 2018  presidential elections it arrested all  candidates running against the president  kind of departed from the targeted  repressive regime as its reconsolidated  what's interesting is that during the  Mubarak period the way that targeted  repression worked was that it was also  co-opting the other groups so while it  was you know arresting the Brotherhood  it was also permitting the leftist to  exist and published their newspaper so I  think they were trying to combat that a  little bit by getting buy-in from  leftists and secular opposition groups  okay so ultimately what do you conclude  yeah so what I conclude you know  academically it focuses quite a bit on  the importance of it you know  considering the legacies of repression  how this conditions political identities  and you know how this very specific  experience of repression can polarize or  you know bring together a political  society I don't know that I have any  policy implications or you know  conclusions more generally it's Egypt is  unfortunately a very sad case at this  point and I think in Tunisia you see as  much as this mattered you know my  argument and polarization and  experiences that created this you know  narrative of joint victimhood it did  matter quite a bit for that initial  period which is crucial for  democratization but it's still politics  so there you know Tunisia continues to  have its own set of of issues friends of  mine from Tunisia you know when I refer  to it as a success story they're like we  still have huge economic problems we  still have security issues so you know  it's a messy process but what I try to  highlight in the book is how these  specific legacies are very important to  consider mm-hmm okay thank you so much  for being here today and sharing some of  your work thank you for more information  about professor Nugent and her research  please visit our website at macmillan  report yale.edu  be sure to join us again for another  episode of the Macmillan report made  possible through funding from the  Whitney and Betty Macmillan sin  for international and area studies at  Yale  you

